  "48": {"title":"Paseo de la Princesa","year":"2023","details":"Viejo San Juan, PR · 35mm","description":"Walls built by colonizers to guard their goods and people from rival invaders now serve to bring in income from tourists","tags":["Film Photography","35mm Film","Color Photography","History","Architecture","Ocean","Social Commentary","Colonial","City Walls","Tourism"]},
  "49": {"title":"Puerto Rico","year":"2023","details":"Viejo San Juan, PR · 35mm","description":"Columbus named the island *San Juan Bautista*. *Puerto Rico* first referred only to a Spanish settlement on one of the Caribbean's best natural harbors. Positioned along the trade-wind route from Spain, it became one of the first major ports ships reached in the Americas, and Spain fortified it heavily with El Morro and San Cristóbal. As the port became the island's main point of contact with the world, sailors, merchants, and mapmakers began calling the whole island by its name. Over the 1500s, the names traded places, as the island became *Puerto Rico* and the city took *San Juan*.","tags":["Film Photography","35mm Film","Color Photography","History","Ocean","Cityscape","Harbor","El Morro","San Cristóbal","Colonial"]},
